   "parent_id": 9098890,
   "author_user_id": 485561,
   "author_display_name": "Mankarse",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/9099177",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2012-02-01T16:11:09Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2012-02-01T16:17:19Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-02-01T16:17:19Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Make sure that every <code>new</code>, <code>env-&gt;NewGlobalRef</code> and <code>jvm-&gt;AttachCurrentThread</code> is in the constructor of an object which calls the matching deallocation function in its destructor.</p>\n\n<p>This is a technique called <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAII\" rel=\"nofollow\">RAII</a>, which is vital to writing any correct program in C++.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 9100268,
   "parent_id": 9098890,
   "author_user_id": 540286,
   "author_display_name": "Ortwin Angermeier",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/9100268",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-02-01T17:21:07Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-02-01T17:21:07Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<ul>\n<li>First try to use <a href=\"http://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_48_0/libs/smart_ptr/smart_ptr.htm\" rel=\"nofollow\">smart pointers</a>. </li>\n<li>As Mankarse pointed out, try to use the RAII idiom whenever possible to create and delete your global references.</li>\n<li>Use as less global rferences as possible</li>\n<li>Free local references when constructing them in a loop</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Take a look at <a href=\"http://java.sun.com/docs/books/jni/html/refs.html\" rel=\"nofollow\">here</a> for managing references.</p>\n\n<p>Do you already know that your native code is leaking memory?</p>\n"
  },
